Employee Salary

class Employee {
    private String name;
    private double salary;

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void setSalary(double salary) {
        if (salary > 0) {
            this.salary = salary;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid salary");
        }
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public double getSalary() {
        return salary;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee e = new Employee();

        e.setName("Arun");
        e.setSalary(25000);

        System.out.println("Name: " + e.getName());
        System.out.println("Salary: " + e.getSalary());

        e.setSalary(-5000);
    }
}

Output

Name: Arun
Salary: 25000.0
Invalid salary

Explanation

private String name: Restricts direct access to the name.

private double salary: Restricts direct access to the salary.

setSalary(): Validates salary before changing it.

getSalary(): Returns the current salary.

Important: Encapsulation does not automatically make data secure. The class must implement appropriate access controls and validation.

3. Advantages of Encapsulation

Protects object data from uncontrolled modification.

Allows validation before values are changed.

Makes code easier to maintain.

Reduces dependency on internal implementation.

Allows internal logic to change without changing the public methods.

4. Abstraction in Detail

Abstraction hides unnecessary implementation details and exposes only the operations users need.

Java provides abstraction through:

Abstract classes

Interfaces

Example: Payment System

abstract class Payment {
    abstract void pay(double amount);

    void showMessage() {
        System.out.println("Payment processing started");
    }
}

class UpiPayment extends Payment {
    @Override
    void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid Rs. " + amount + " using UPI");
    }
}

class CardPayment extends Payment {
    @Override
    void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid Rs. " + amount + " using Card");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Payment p = new UpiPayment();

        p.showMessage();
        p.pay(500);
    }
}

Output

Payment processing started
Paid Rs. 500.0 using UPI

Explanation

Payment defines a common payment operation.

pay() is abstract, so subclasses provide the implementation.

UpiPayment implements payment using UPI.

CardPayment implements payment using a card.

The calling code can work with the common Payment type.
